WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:01.490
- You know her from so many greats,

00:00:01.490 --> 00:00:03.680
like "ER," "The Good
Wife," and "The Sopranos,"

00:00:03.680 --> 00:00:05.290
just to name a few.

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:07.540
Her new book, "Sunshine
Girl: An Unexpected Life"

00:00:07.540 --> 00:00:08.610
is out right now.

00:00:08.610 --> 00:00:11.633
Please, everybody welcome
Julianna Margulies!

00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:16.633
(audience cheers and claps)
(warm rock music)

00:00:22.234 --> 00:00:23.070
- [Kelly] COVID hug.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.920
I know, we can't hug each
other, it's so weird.

00:00:24.920 --> 00:00:26.010
- It's so, so weird.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:26.843
- It's so weird, I know.

00:00:26.843 --> 00:00:28.900
And I just, like, doing
certain things on the show,

00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:31.550
it's just still, I'm still
getting used to it, but anyway,

00:00:31.550 --> 00:00:33.297
what is mother's Day like for you?

00:00:33.297 --> 00:00:34.130
(Julianna laughs)
Yes!

00:00:34.130 --> 00:00:34.963
- Let's jump right in.

00:00:34.963 --> 00:00:36.380
- [Kelly] Let's do it, we
were just talking about it.

00:00:36.380 --> 00:00:39.830
- Mother's Day, well, I
think this year especially,

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:43.660
we should all be just
praising all the mothers,

00:00:43.660 --> 00:00:45.820
because it is just known that
(audience applauding)

00:00:45.820 --> 00:00:48.204
most of the work fell on
mothers during COVID, you know?

00:00:48.204 --> 00:00:49.037
- Yeah, yeah.

00:00:49.037 --> 00:00:50.320
- And in the pandemic, look at that,

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:52.124
everyone's like, yes.
- Yeah.

00:00:52.124 --> 00:00:56.290
- And I can't complain 'cause
I have a very lovely life.

00:00:56.290 --> 00:01:00.000
So, even though I was having
moments of complaining,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.511
because of the laundry, and
the cooking, and the shopping,

00:01:01.511 --> 00:01:02.344
and the cleaning-

00:01:02.344 --> 00:01:03.177
- All of it is invalidated.

00:01:03.177 --> 00:01:04.010
- All of that stuff.
- Yeah.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:05.500
- There are so many
incredible women out there

00:01:05.500 --> 00:01:08.860
who are single mothers,
who lost their jobs.

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:10.480
Like, I just feel like this year,

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:13.518
that we should all just
celebrate every mother.

00:01:13.518 --> 00:01:14.351
That's how I feel.
(audience cheers and claps)

00:01:14.351 --> 00:01:15.540
- [Kelly] And that we've made it.

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:17.290
- Yeah, yeah.
(Kelly laughs)

00:01:17.290 --> 00:01:18.123
- My girl.

00:01:18.123 --> 00:01:19.763
- But my favorite, you
know, I'm not a big,

00:01:21.500 --> 00:01:23.310
I mean, of course, it's always
nice to get a little present,

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:26.460
but really, for me, all I
want is a card from my kid.

00:01:26.460 --> 00:01:27.293
- Me too.

00:01:27.293 --> 00:01:28.540
- And coffee in bed.

00:01:28.540 --> 00:01:30.660
- Oh, I, well, my kids are far too young

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:31.493
to do that. (laughs)

00:01:31.493 --> 00:01:33.300
- Yes, so I started teaching my son

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:35.770
how to make coffee for me around seven.

00:01:35.770 --> 00:01:37.520
- Oh, my daughter's about
must be seven, here we go.

00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:38.540
- Yeah, no, start now.
(audience laughs)

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:39.640
- [Kelly] Okay, okay.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:41.630
- Then, you know, if you're
a little anal retentive

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:45.230
like I am, you might not want
them to bring you the coffee

00:01:45.230 --> 00:01:46.810
until they're a little older. (laughs)

00:01:46.810 --> 00:01:47.880
- Oh, 'cause they-
- 'Cause they'll spill it.

00:01:47.880 --> 00:01:48.830
- Unless it has a lid, okay.

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:49.663
- Unless it has a lid.

00:01:49.663 --> 00:01:51.760
But to me, sitting in
bed with a cup of coffee

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.080
on Mother's Day with my
husband, and my kid, and my dog,

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.980
that's my idea of a perfect Mother's Day.

00:01:55.980 --> 00:01:57.977
- Yeah, I get it, just relaxing
and not having to move.

00:01:57.977 --> 00:02:00.400
- Yeah, and not having
to move, or wash a dish.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.110
- Amen, so, well, you're son, Kieran, he,

00:02:03.110 --> 00:02:04.437
is he about to be 13, or he is 13?

00:02:04.437 --> 00:02:06.763
- Kieran just turned 13 in January.

00:02:08.299 --> 00:02:09.132
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:02:09.132 --> 00:02:10.747
- [Julianna] He turned 13,
teenager, I have to say-

00:02:10.747 --> 00:02:12.640
- So, I have the five-year-old
boy that just turned five,

00:02:12.640 --> 00:02:14.900
So what do I have to look
forward to with teenagers?

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:17.260
- Oh, you know, well, he's
still at a great stage.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:19.360
I don't know any further than 13, but-

00:02:19.360 --> 00:02:20.193
- [Kelly] Okay.

00:02:20.193 --> 00:02:24.530
- One thing I'll say is,
I know, and most moms,

00:02:24.530 --> 00:02:27.410
I don't know why boys are so smelly.

00:02:27.410 --> 00:02:28.243
- Oh.
(audience laughs)

00:02:28.243 --> 00:02:31.400
- The smell starts happening in the feet,

00:02:31.400 --> 00:02:34.996
the BO, it's shocking.
(audience laughs)

00:02:34.996 --> 00:02:35.829
- [Kelly] Oh.

00:02:35.829 --> 00:02:37.900
- In our house, we were
like, Kieran, you're rank.

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:39.380
- I am sensitive to smells, wow.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:42.450
- Yeah, well, when their
hormones are going,

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:44.640
those smells come up really fast.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:46.100
So that's something you
can look forward to.

00:02:46.100 --> 00:02:46.933
- Mm.

00:02:46.933 --> 00:02:50.060
- The other thing though
is conversation, you know?

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:50.893
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:02:50.893 --> 00:02:53.550
- When they're this age, I mean, really,

00:02:53.550 --> 00:02:57.590
from the age of 10 on, but
at 13, he's learning so much,

00:02:57.590 --> 00:03:00.980
and dinner conversation
just becomes a different,

00:03:00.980 --> 00:03:02.200
a different experience.

00:03:02.200 --> 00:03:03.120
- A little elevated?

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.950
- It's elevated, and we
have topics of conversation,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:07.470
and especially during COVID,

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.000
when we were together
every night, which is rare

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:12.760
for most families to have
both parents home for dinner,

00:03:12.760 --> 00:03:13.940
and you know-
- Yeah.

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.810
- We would pick, we would all say,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:17.960
what did you learn new
about your day, you know?

00:03:17.960 --> 00:03:19.580
That was his idea.

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:20.770
And I was like, I love it, okay,

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:21.950
this is what I learned, you know?

00:03:21.950 --> 00:03:25.100
And just, there's an ease of conversation

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:29.030
and communication that I
find really refreshing,

00:03:29.030 --> 00:03:31.430
but he hasn't hit the
I hate you stage yet.

00:03:31.430 --> 00:03:33.670
And so, when that happens, I'll
come back and let you know.

00:03:33.670 --> 00:03:37.100
- Yeah, I love that your
kid, we thought of that up.

00:03:37.100 --> 00:03:38.500
My ex and I, like, when we were together,

00:03:38.500 --> 00:03:39.760
we thought up at the dinner table,

00:03:39.760 --> 00:03:41.360
like saying one positive thing,

00:03:41.360 --> 00:03:43.980
and one thing that's not negative,
but you'd like to change.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:45.670
It is like pulling teeth.

00:03:45.670 --> 00:03:47.160
Like, it's like with my
four-year-old, he's like,

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.400
I'm like, what did you like about today?

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:50.070
And he's like, I don't know, I don't know.

00:03:50.070 --> 00:03:51.643
He's already a teenager, I don't know.

00:03:51.643 --> 00:03:53.480
(audience laughs)
I'm like, okay, cool, thanks.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:55.810
- Yeah, that's boys too,
you'll say, how was school?

00:03:55.810 --> 00:03:56.643
Good.

00:03:56.643 --> 00:03:57.476
What'd you do?

00:03:57.476 --> 00:03:58.309
Stuff.

00:03:58.309 --> 00:03:59.142
- Yeah, I don't know.

00:03:59.142 --> 00:04:00.090
- They don't expand.

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.180
Girls are like, oh, well let me, you know,

00:04:01.180 --> 00:04:02.687
they're so much more
communicative at a younger age.

00:04:02.687 --> 00:04:04.020
- She goes into details.
- Yeah.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:05.930
- Well, we actually started
out, we got in a fight,

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:07.570
but now we're good, and
we're best friends again.

00:04:07.570 --> 00:04:08.650
It's like a whole thing.

00:04:08.650 --> 00:04:09.530
Anyway, they are different.

00:04:09.530 --> 00:04:11.350
It's another rad mom hour again.

00:04:11.350 --> 00:04:14.406
Give it up for Julianna
Margulies and my band, y'all!

00:04:14.406 --> 00:04:16.015
(audience claps and cheers)

00:04:16.015 --> 00:04:18.810
So, their mamas are here all show long,

00:04:18.810 --> 00:04:20.640
in honor of Mother's Day.

00:04:20.640 --> 00:04:22.070
So we've got Stephanie over there,

00:04:22.070 --> 00:04:25.707
who I've known for a
very long time, ma'am.

00:04:25.707 --> 00:04:27.750
(laughs) 'Cause Jason's
been with me since "Idol,"

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:30.970
so, she is the mom of
my band leader, Jason.

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.560
Hi Stephanie!

00:04:32.560 --> 00:04:34.890
- Hi Kelly, thanks for having us.

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:37.320
- Oh, I love you, I
love you coming on here.

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:39.580
So wait, you two look
so much alike though.

00:04:39.580 --> 00:04:41.410
I've always thought this.

00:04:41.410 --> 00:04:43.700
- Well, actually, we
do, you're right, Kelly.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.580
I said, but I always say,
I'm the one with hair.

00:04:46.580 --> 00:04:51.310
That way you know-
(audience laughing)

00:04:51.310 --> 00:04:54.410
- So wait, did Jason tell you not

00:04:54.410 --> 00:04:56.430
to say anything that might embarrass him?

00:04:56.430 --> 00:04:58.940
'Cause I wanna know that thing. (laughs)

00:04:58.940 --> 00:05:01.320
- Okay, well, he did text me,

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:04.280
and he reminded me it was
"The Kelly Clarkson Show"

00:05:04.280 --> 00:05:05.976
and not "The Jason Halbert Show."

00:05:05.976 --> 00:05:09.380
(audience laughing)

00:05:09.380 --> 00:05:11.280
Well, he thinks he's got

00:05:11.280 --> 00:05:13.640
the worst singing voice in the world.

00:05:13.640 --> 00:05:15.000
- He does not.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:17.120
- Yes, I agree, I totally agree with you,

00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:19.690
I totally disagree with him.

00:05:19.690 --> 00:05:21.910
I'm always asking him,
why don't you sing more?

00:05:21.910 --> 00:05:25.280
His voice is unique, I tell
him it's like Rod Stewart.

00:05:26.163 --> 00:05:28.410
And I do believe I've
told everybody in the band

00:05:28.410 --> 00:05:30.470
that Jason needs to sing more.

00:05:30.470 --> 00:05:34.133
So, Jason, please sing
for your mama. (laughs)

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:36.027
- Oh!
- No, nope.

00:05:36.027 --> 00:05:38.520
- So, okay, what I got from this is that

00:05:38.520 --> 00:05:40.830
we need to do a duet, Jason, on the show.

00:05:40.830 --> 00:05:42.493
- Okay, yeah. (laughing)

00:05:42.493 --> 00:05:43.326
- Yes!
(audience applauding)

00:05:43.326 --> 00:05:45.060
That's what I heard, Alice!

00:05:45.060 --> 00:05:45.950
- Season 10!

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:48.640
- That's what I heard,
that's what I heard.

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:50.730
That's coming for you, mama!

00:05:50.730 --> 00:05:54.807
I love this right now, his
face is so red, Stephanie.

00:05:54.807 --> 00:05:57.510
(audience laughing)

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:58.580
- Mission accomplished.

00:05:58.580 --> 00:06:00.170
- Mission accomplished!

00:06:00.170 --> 00:06:01.350
All right, all right.
(audience applauding)

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.917
Well Julianna, so, is your
son, do you embarrass him?

00:06:03.917 --> 00:06:04.913
But do you ever?

00:06:07.200 --> 00:06:08.830
- He says I don't embarrass him.

00:06:08.830 --> 00:06:11.453
I will threaten to
embarrass him all the time.

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.380
- [Kelly] Keep him in line.

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:14.320
- Just to keep him in line,

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:15.610
the only thing that embarrasses him

00:06:15.610 --> 00:06:18.670
is when he was like three,

00:06:18.670 --> 00:06:20.730
we got a video of him
running around the yard

00:06:20.730 --> 00:06:22.520
with his sun hat on, totally naked,

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:26.000
chasing after his grandparents' dog,

00:06:26.000 --> 00:06:28.060
and with just everything
hanging out, and I have it.

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:31.231
And I always say, just know
that I will play that video.

00:06:31.231 --> 00:06:33.862
- Promise this.
- For sure, at some point.

00:06:33.862 --> 00:06:34.695
(audience laughing)

00:06:34.695 --> 00:06:35.920
Like, either your-
- Graduation. (laughs)

00:06:35.920 --> 00:06:38.240
- Graduation or your wedding or something.

00:06:38.240 --> 00:06:40.820
But I actually said to him
the other day, I was like,

00:06:40.820 --> 00:06:42.320
would it embarrass you
if, I don't know what,

00:06:42.320 --> 00:06:44.040
I was doing something in the, it,

00:06:44.040 --> 00:06:45.340
we were walking in the street in New York,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:48.550
and he goes, mom, no, you're in a mask.

00:06:48.550 --> 00:06:49.799
Like, right now.
(audience laughs)

00:06:49.799 --> 00:06:50.632
- Like, yeah.

00:06:50.632 --> 00:06:51.940
- Nothing embarrasses him.
- Nobody knows.

00:06:51.940 --> 00:06:53.730
- The only thing that upsets him is

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:56.100
if anyone's ever rude to people.

00:06:56.100 --> 00:06:56.933
(audience aws)
- Oh.

00:06:56.933 --> 00:06:57.950
- [Julianna] He gets very upset.

00:06:57.950 --> 00:06:58.820
- That's great!

00:06:58.820 --> 00:07:01.273
- Yeah, and calls them all Karens.

00:07:01.273 --> 00:07:04.198
(audience laughing)

00:07:04.198 --> 00:07:06.490
He was like, oh my God, that
we spoke to that taxi driver,

00:07:06.490 --> 00:07:07.700
she's such a Karen.

00:07:07.700 --> 00:07:08.610
- Oh my gosh!

00:07:08.610 --> 00:07:09.960
- And I didn't know what Karen was

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.260
until he taught me two years ago.

00:07:12.260 --> 00:07:13.890
I was like, what, what's a Karen?

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:14.723
I didn't know.

00:07:14.723 --> 00:07:16.520
- Oh my gosh, I know.

00:07:16.520 --> 00:07:18.517
- He'll let, he's like you
better never be a Karen.

00:07:18.517 --> 00:07:19.530
And I was like, I'm not, I'm not, I'm not!

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:21.065
- [Kelly] Then you will embarrass him,

00:07:21.065 --> 00:07:21.898
then you will embarrass him.

00:07:21.898 --> 00:07:22.731
- Yeah, then I would embarrass him.

00:07:22.731 --> 00:07:24.490
- Well, your new book, it's
called, "Sunshine Girl,"

00:07:24.490 --> 00:07:26.160
so, why "Sunshine Girl?"

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:30.780
- So, Sunshine Girl was a
nickname my mother gave me

00:07:30.780 --> 00:07:34.060
when I was pretty much
right out of the womb,

00:07:34.060 --> 00:07:36.390
because I didn't cry.
- Oh.

00:07:36.390 --> 00:07:39.660
- I was very, I was just
very peaceful and smiley,

00:07:39.660 --> 00:07:43.150
and she used to say
she'd come into my room

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.360
at nine in the morning
'cause I never woke her up.

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:47.080
Like, you know, and I'd be standing there,

00:07:47.080 --> 00:07:48.610
wet from head to toe, just smiling.

00:07:48.610 --> 00:07:50.910
So, she called me her Sunshine Girl.

00:07:50.910 --> 00:07:55.010
And I ruminated a lot on that title.

00:07:55.010 --> 00:07:57.410
But it made sense to me,
because throughout the book,

00:07:57.410 --> 00:08:00.830
I talk about how it's real disservice

00:08:00.830 --> 00:08:04.930
to label your children,
because it was a nickname

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.780
that was out of love, but
it was crippling for me,

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:10.470
because I couldn't be anything else,

00:08:10.470 --> 00:08:12.940
because I thought that's
what was expected of me.

00:08:12.940 --> 00:08:13.773
- Right.

00:08:13.773 --> 00:08:15.230
- So with all the difficult
stuff that happened,

00:08:15.230 --> 00:08:18.190
I was the one who felt like
I had to cheer everyone up,

00:08:18.190 --> 00:08:19.850
and make everyone feel better.

00:08:19.850 --> 00:08:20.750
- [Kelly] 'Cause you're a Sunshine Girl.

00:08:20.750 --> 00:08:22.880
- And be like, oh no, we're moving where?

00:08:22.880 --> 00:08:23.910
And we're living, I'm good.

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:25.520
You know, like, never having that.

00:08:25.520 --> 00:08:27.290
And I brought it into my adult life,

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.290
where I didn't know how to
set boundaries to say no,

00:08:29.290 --> 00:08:31.430
that actually that, I
didn't wanna be difficult,

00:08:31.430 --> 00:08:32.670
because I was the Sunshine Girl.

00:08:32.670 --> 00:08:35.040
- We are so similar!
- Yeah.

00:08:35.040 --> 00:08:37.610
- Yeah, and I was like,
wow, I'm in therapy.

00:08:37.610 --> 00:08:38.443
- Yeah.
(audience laughs)

00:08:38.443 --> 00:08:40.750
- I was like, no, it's
so true that you're not,

00:08:40.750 --> 00:08:43.080
I felt like that too, I was
always the kid to be like, Ugh.

00:08:43.080 --> 00:08:43.913
- Yeah-
- Like I just wanna

00:08:43.913 --> 00:08:46.240
make everyone, I don't wanna
make any anybody uncomfortable.

00:08:46.240 --> 00:08:48.470
I don't wanna, that's a lot of kids

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:49.540
growing up in those environments.

00:08:49.540 --> 00:08:52.500
- Yeah, and to the other
effect too, you know,

00:08:52.500 --> 00:08:55.700
with you saying, oh,
that's my melancholy kid.

00:08:55.700 --> 00:08:58.170
Oh, she was always so melancholy,
they'll grow into that

00:08:58.170 --> 00:08:59.420
if that's what you-
- You put that on them,

00:08:59.420 --> 00:09:00.270
you project that on them.

00:09:00.270 --> 00:09:01.760
- Yeah, so I feel like,
it's one of the reasons

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.360
why I loved Michelle Obama's book title,

00:09:04.360 --> 00:09:07.060
called "Becoming," because
she talks in her book

00:09:07.060 --> 00:09:10.440
about let your children
become, don't ask them

00:09:10.440 --> 00:09:13.320
what they wanna be when they
grow up when they're seven.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:15.280
Let them become that
person they're gonna be.

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:18.610
And the same with nicknames,
as much as you think it's cute,

00:09:18.610 --> 00:09:21.110
you know, it stays with
you in different ways,

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:22.280
and it comes out.

00:09:22.280 --> 00:09:25.170
I was really, it was quite
cathartic to write this book,

00:09:25.170 --> 00:09:27.750
because I got to dive in pretty deep.

00:09:27.750 --> 00:09:28.583
(Kelly mmhmms)

00:09:28.583 --> 00:09:30.010
And I realized, I was like, wow,

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:33.650
I didn't really start drawing
boundaries till I was 35.

00:09:33.650 --> 00:09:36.640
- Oh my God, I'm 38, and
just now figuring that out.

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:38.520
So, I feel better.
(Julianna and crowd laugh)

00:09:38.520 --> 00:09:42.061
Yeah, I'm like, I'm just
saying, it's very hard.

00:09:42.061 --> 00:09:42.894
- It's hard to change that narrative.

00:09:42.894 --> 00:09:44.350
- When you have a life of habitual, like,

00:09:44.350 --> 00:09:46.350
patterns that you, yeah.
- Yeah.

00:09:46.350 --> 00:09:48.590
- Especially if they're like
embedded from childhood.

00:09:48.590 --> 00:09:49.497
- Because it's what you know.

00:09:49.497 --> 00:09:52.990
- And you think you're trying
and you're doing your best,

00:09:52.990 --> 00:09:54.160
and you think you're being compassionate,

00:09:54.160 --> 00:09:55.280
and you think you're
doing all these things,

00:09:55.280 --> 00:09:57.060
and really, you're doing the opposite.

00:09:57.060 --> 00:09:58.102
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:09:58.102 --> 00:10:00.060
- I mean, listen, no one wants
to hang out with a martyr.

00:10:00.060 --> 00:10:01.530
You know?
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:10:01.530 --> 00:10:02.363
- It's not really fun.

00:10:02.363 --> 00:10:04.190
- And no one wants to be with
you either, it's exhausting.

00:10:04.190 --> 00:10:05.690
So!
(both laugh)

00:10:05.690 --> 00:10:06.610
- [Julianna] It's exhausting.

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:08.077
- So, Julianna's new book,

00:10:08.077 --> 00:10:11.160
"Sunshine Girl: An Enexpected
Life," is out right now.

00:10:11.160 --> 00:10:12.537
You gotta check it out.

